      "writtenOn": "2023-08-07T10:04:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What driver needs these symbols?  They are not even present in newer kernel releases, so you can not rely on them being around anymore at all.",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1237ff0f_449c3c52",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1886360
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-08T01:50:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For kernel 5.15+, we can use xa_for_each() following the change: \n\n\"a33df75c63 block: use an xarray for disk-\u003epart_tbl\" , which modify structure as:\n-\t/* Array of pointers to partitions indexed by partno.\n-\t * Protected with matching bdev lock but stat and other\n-\t * non-critical accesses use RCU.  Always access through\n-\t * helpers.\n-\t */\n-\tstruct disk_part_tbl __rcu *part_tbl;\n+\tstruct xarray part_tbl;\n \tstruct block_device *part0;\n\nBut our driver(eMMC/UFS write protect) can use disk_part_iter_xxx symbol instead of xa_for_each() to iterate partitions in one disk for kernel 5.10.",
      "parentUuid": "0efbb5f4_06e1ec64",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "742492c3_dbd3e8f8",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-08T04:47:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why would a driver need to ever iterate the partitions?  No in-kernel driver does this, so it seems very odd that this would ever be a normal operation.",
      "parentUuid": "1237ff0f_449c3c52",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c4780bd_9a08af9c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-08T04:48:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "do you have a pointer to the source of this driver so that we can review it to provide an idea of what should be done instead?",
      "parentUuid": "742492c3_dbd3e8f8",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "344b5060_46583877",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1886360
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-12T00:52:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "MTK has newly developed a general method for setting read-only partitions. \n\nWhen the kernel starts, the cmdline parameter contains information about the address and length of the read-only partition\n(For example: mtk_wp.wp_user\u003d0x7c000,0xbc000,\nblock address \u003d 0x7c000, length \u003d 0xbc000),\n\nAnd mtk-wp.ko will parse these read-only block information\n(Traverse all partitions of the block device to set the partition as read only if it is in the protected range).",
      "parentUuid": "6c4780bd_9a08af9c",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a92d1607_dc60c06d",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-15T16:11:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Parsing all of this should be done in userspace, not in the kernel, have your init process scripts handle this, the kernel should NOT be doing this for obvious reasons (i.e. this is a new user/kernel API you are creating that is not upstream.)",
      "parentUuid": "344b5060_46583877",
      "revId": "b22fb3f6213ee72196577132193521ee0f04ebdb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
